<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Solution-wide defaults -->
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <!-- Warnings are tolerant locally; CI passes /p:TreatWarningsAsErrors=true -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarningLevel>5</WarningLevel>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <!-- .NET platform analyzers on for every project in the solution -->
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Versioning (backlog E.1).

    Until this existed, no version was set at all, so every assembly carried the
    SDK default and there was no way to tell one build from another. A bug report
    saying "the resume duplicate is back" could not be tied to a build, and
    the change history had no version numbers to hang its entries on.

    Bump VersionPrefix here and nowhere else — every project inherits it.

      MAJOR  a breaking change to the public API of Core, Games or Hosting.
             api/*.api.txt is the record of what that surface is;
             PublicApiSurfaceTests fails when it moves, so you will know.
      MINOR  new modes, new decks, new capability.
      PATCH  fixes only.

    1.0 as of August 2026. The 0.x latitude is spent: from here a breaking
    change to the public surface of Core, Games or Hosting requires a MAJOR
    bump, not a quiet edit.

    api/*.api.txt is the record of that surface and PublicApiSurfaceTests
    fails when it moves, so the signal is automatic. Reading the diff:

      REMOVED           breaking for anyone referencing it. MAJOR *if* these
                        assemblies are ever published. They are not: nothing
                        sets IsPackable, no PackageReference to TableTop exists
                        anywhere, and the only consumers are this repo's own
                        heads via ProjectReference. So a removal that breaks
                        nothing in-tree is MINOR — and the entry below must say
                        what was removed and why it was safe.

                        This carve-out is deliberate and narrow. It exists
                        because the alternative was a 2.0.0 for deleting an
                        unreachable duplicate game mode, which would make the
                        major number meaningless. The real safety mechanism is
                        PublicApiSurfaceTests, which fails on ANY removal and
                        forces this decision to be conscious — it did exactly
                        that when TimelineMode was deleted in 1.8.0. Publish a
                        package and this row reverts to a flat MAJOR.
      ADDED to a class  new capability. MINOR.
      ADDED to an
      INTERFACE         breaking for anyone who implements it, even though it
                        reads as an addition. MAJOR — unless it carries a
                        default implementation.
      ADDED to an
      ENUM              MINOR by .NET convention, but audit every switch on
                        that enum before believing it.

    That last row is not theoretical. Adding MonogamyZone.Fantasy in 1.5.0 broke
    two exhaustive switches inside this very repository — JsonCardProvider's zone
    parser and DeckFileValidator's zone check — and neither failed loudly. The
    parser silently dropped every card in the new zone (file had 118, game loaded
    106, no error anywhere), and the validator would have rejected them all as
    invalid. A consumer switching on one of our enums can break exactly the same
    way, so "MINOR" here means the version number, not the amount of care.

    That third case is easy to miss: `ICardTurnController` gained `Players`
    immediately after 1.0 was declared, and the diff called it an addition. A
    default implementation was considered and rejected — returning an empty
    roster would have made the flow controls silently no-op, which is worse than
    a compile error.

    VersionSuffix is empty for a release build. CI can pass
    /p:VersionSuffix=beta.1 or similar for a pre-release without editing this.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VersionPrefix>1.28.0</VersionPrefix>
    <VersionSuffix></VersionSuffix>

    <!--
      AssemblyVersion stays on the major only, so it is 1.0.0.0 for the whole
      1.x line and does NOT follow VersionPrefix. It is the identity the runtime
      binds against, so moving it on every patch would force a rebuild of
      anything referencing the engine for no benefit. FileVersion and
      InformationalVersion carry the precise build.
    -->
    <AssemblyVersion>1.0.0.0</AssemblyVersion>

    <!--
      FileVersion must be set explicitly. It defaults to AssemblyVersion, and
      since AssemblyVersion is pinned to the major, leaving it alone meant every
      1.x build reported 1.0.0.0 as its file version — exactly the "can't tell
      one build from another" problem this whole block exists to solve, quietly
      reintroduced by the pinning. Deriving it from VersionPrefix keeps it
      tracking without a second number to remember.
    -->
    <FileVersion>$(VersionPrefix).0</FileVersion>

    <Authors>TableTop</Authors>
    <Product>TableTop</Product>
    <Copyright>Copyright © TableTop</Copyright>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>

    <!-- Deterministic already set above; this makes the source link reproducible too. -->
    <IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>true</IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
